  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nCurrently have strong upper ridging centered over the Four\nCorners region with upper troughing from Ontario down through\nthe Great Lakes region. Meanwhile, there is a stationary front\nnear or just south of the KS/NE border with high pressure from\nthe Mississippi Valley through the Southeast CONUS. \n\nUpper ridging will continue to expand into the Southern Plains\ntoday as the surface high also slides east. This will allow the\nfront to lift north and start to wash out. There will be a\nslight chance for a few storms to develop late this afternoon\nover northwest KS along an area of surface convergence which\nwill be in a highly mixed airmass. If storms do develop, they\nwould mainly affect a few counties in central KS after 00z Sun. \n\nConfidence is high in above normal temps area wide today with a\nfew locations west of I-135 reaching 100. How much we mix today\nwill determine how extreme heat indices get today. Currently\nhave a lean to the drier more mixed solution for locations along\nand especially west of I-135, which would result in heat index\nvalues near or a couple degrees higher than temps. Hotter\ntemperatures are still likely for Sunday and will continue\nthrough Wednesday. Through this period highs will generally be\nin the 98-104 degree range, with the higher temps over southern\nand central KS. Models continue the trend of mixing dewpoints\nover western portions of our forecast area into the 50s each\nafternoon, which will keep heat indices from getting too \nextreme. However, even with a drier airmass in place, heat \nindices will still be in the 102-107 range for 3-4 days, so a \nheat headline may still be needed. \n\nFor Wed into Thu the upper pattern will undergo a change, with \none piece of energy tracking southeast out of British Columbia \nwith another impulse sliding southeast out of MB. This will \nallow a cold front to move into the area for Wed night into Thu \nand will bring cooler readings and increased storm chances. The \nGFS pushes the front through faster compared to the ECMWF, so \nconfidence in the exact timing of the front is not that high at\nthis time.",
